     {q:"Which of these is an example of a pun?", options:["She ran quickly to catch the bus.","The sun rose over the quiet hills.","The tall building had many windows.","I used to be a baker, but I could not make enough dough."], answer:3},
     {q:"Why might a pun rely on a word having more than one meaning?", options:["Puns never depend on a words multiple meanings","The double meaning creates the surprising or humorous connection","A pun always uses only one clear meaning of a word","This concept has no connection to figurative language"], answer:1},
     {q:"Why might an author use wordplay like a pun in a piece of writing?", options:["This concept has no relevance to figurative language","Puns are never intended to be humorous","To add humour or cleverness that engages the reader","Wordplay always makes writing more confusing and unclear"], answer:2},
     {q:"What skill helps a reader understand and enjoy a pun?", options:["Recognizing multiple meanings or similar sounds in words","Puns can be understood without any knowledge of word meanings","Ignoring the meaning of every word in a sentence","This concept has no connection to reading"], answer:0}
